{
 "id": "17.2",
 "module": "m54047",
 "chapter": "17",
 "title": "Nonhomogeneous Linear Equations",
 "short": "Nonhomogeneous Linear Equations",
 "lead": "A <span data-concept=\"nonhomogeneous-linear-equation\">nonhomogeneous equation</span> keeps a term with no y in it. This section writes its general solution from the solution of its <span data-concept=\"complementary-equation\">complementary equation</span> and one <span data-concept=\"particular-solution\">particular solution</span>, and finds that particular solution two ways: by the <span data-concept=\"method-of-undetermined-coefficients\">method of undetermined coefficients</span> and by the <span data-concept=\"method-of-variation-of-parameters\">method of variation of parameters</span>, with <span data-concept=\"cramers-rule\">Cramer’s rule</span> to solve its system.",
 "objectives": [
  "Write the general solution to a nonhomogeneous differential equation.",
  "Solve a nonhomogeneous differential equation by the method of undetermined coefficients.",
  "Solve a nonhomogeneous differential equation by the method of variation of parameters."
 ],
 "summary_html": "<ul><li>To solve a nonhomogeneous linear second-order <span data-concept=\"differential-equation\">differential equation</span>, first find the <span data-concept=\"general-solution\">general solution</span> to the <span data-concept=\"complementary-equation\">complementary equation</span>, then find a <span data-concept=\"particular-solution\">particular solution</span> to the <span data-concept=\"nonhomogeneous-linear-equation\">nonhomogeneous equation</span>.</li><li>Let $\\kyp(x)$ be any <span data-concept=\"particular-solution\">particular solution</span> to the <span data-concept=\"nonhomogeneous-linear-equation\">nonhomogeneous linear differential equation</span> $$a_{2}(x)\\kydprime+a_{1}(x)\\kyprime+a_{0}(x)\\kyfn=\\krfn(x),$$ and let $c_{1}\\kyone(x)+c_{2}\\kytwo(x)$ denote the <span data-concept=\"general-solution\">general solution</span> to the <span data-concept=\"complementary-equation\">complementary equation</span>. Then, the <span data-concept=\"general-solution-to-a-nonhomogeneous-equation\">general solution to the nonhomogeneous equation</span> is given by $$\\kyfn(x)=c_{1}\\kyone(x)+c_{2}\\kytwo(x)+\\kyp(x\\text{).}$$</li><li>When $\\krfn(x)$ is a combination of polynomials, <span data-concept=\"exponential-function\">exponential functions</span>, sines, and cosines, use the <span data-concept=\"method-of-undetermined-coefficients\">method of undetermined coefficients</span> to find the <span data-concept=\"particular-solution\">particular solution</span>. To use this method, assume a solution in the same form as $\\krfn(x),$ multiplying by <em>x</em> as necessary until the assumed solution is <span data-concept=\"linear-independence\">linearly independent</span> of the <span data-concept=\"general-solution\">general solution</span> to the <span data-concept=\"complementary-equation\">complementary equation</span>. Then, substitute the assumed solution into the <span data-concept=\"differential-equation\">differential equation</span> to find values for the coefficients.</li><li>When $\\krfn(x)$ is <em>not</em> a combination of polynomials, <span data-concept=\"exponential-function\">exponential functions</span>, or sines and cosines, use the <span data-concept=\"method-of-variation-of-parameters\">method of variation of parameters</span> to find the <span data-concept=\"particular-solution\">particular solution</span>. This method involves using <span data-concept=\"cramers-rule\">Cramer’s rule</span> or another suitable technique to find functions $\\kuprime(x)$ and $\\kvprime(x)$ satisfying $$\\begin{array}{rll}\\kuprime\\kyone+\\kvprime\\kytwo & = & 0 \\\\ \\kuprime\\kyoneprime+\\kvprime\\kytwoprime & = & \\krfn(x\\text{).}\\end{array}$$ Then, $\\kyp(x)=\\kufn(x)\\kyone(x)+\\kv(x)\\kytwo(x)$ is a particular <span data-concept=\"solution-to-a-differential-equation\">solution to the differential equation</span>.</li></ul>",
